    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "The TLB",
      "question": "Paging adds a memory lookup to every access. Why isn't everything twice as slow?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "TLBs, question one. Paging adds a page table lookup to every memory access. So why isn't every program twice as slow? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Paging adds a lookup to every access. Why isn't it <em>2× slower</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "The TLB, the translation lookaside buffer, is a small cache of recent translations, inside the CPU. Sum an array of ten ints, on tiny sixteen byte pages. Element zero misses: the page table is walked, and the translation for page six is cached. Elements one and two hit. Element three is on a new page: a miss. Then three hits. Element seven misses, and the last two hit. Three misses, seven hits: a seventy percent hit rate, just from spatial locality. With real four kilobyte pages, one miss covers a thousand ints. And loops that come back hit again, from temporal locality.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Hits from <em>locality</em>",
