    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "TLBs and context switches",
      "question": "After a context switch, the TLB still holds the old process's translations. What can the OS do?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. After a context switch, the TLB still holds the old process's translations. What can the OS do about them? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "After a switch, the TLB holds <em>stale</em> translations. Now what?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Virtual page ten maps to different frames in different processes, so stale entries would be wrong. The simple fix is to flush the TLB on every switch, and the new process starts cold. The better fix is to tag each entry with an address space I D: A S I D on ARM, P C I D on x86. Entries from several processes coexist, only matching tags hit, and a process that runs again soon finds its translations still there. Either way, kernel mappings, identical in every process, are marked global, and survive.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Flush, or <em>tag</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "flush",
              "label": "Flush",
              "title": "Simple, starts cold",
              "body": "Invalidate everything on each switch; every process re-misses."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "tag",
              "label": "Tag (ASID / PCID)",
              "title": "Entries coexist",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "Only matching tags hit; returning processes find warm entries."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "global",
              "text": "Kernel mappings are global: they survive either way.",
              "code": "PTE G bit"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Changing a mapping is the other case. After unmapping memory, or changing its protection, the kernel must invalidate those entries. But other CPUs, running threads of the same process, may have cached them too. So the kernel interrupts each of those CPUs, and makes it invalidate its own copy. That's a TLB shootdown, and it's why unmapping memory in a busy multithreaded program is surprisingly expensive.",
          "props": {
            "title": "TLB <em>shootdowns</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "unmapping",
                "label": "Change a mapping",
                "title": "Invalidate it",
                "body": "munmap, mprotect, page migration.",
                "code": "invlpg"
              },
              {
                "at": "cached",
                "label": "Other CPUs",
                "title": "Have copies too",
                "body": "Threads of the same process, elsewhere.",
                "code": "shared mm"
              },
              {
                "at": "interrupts",
                "label": "Shootdown",
                "title": "Interrupt each one",
                "body": "An IPI to every CPU that may hold it.",
                "code": "and wait for them"
              },
              {
                "at": "expensive",
                "label": "Cost",
                "title": "Surprisingly expensive",
                "body": "Batch unmaps; avoid churn in hot paths.",
                "code": "munmap is not free"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Multi-level page tables",
      "question": "A flat page table is 4 MB per process. How do real systems make it smaller?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. A flat page table for a 32 bit address space takes four megabytes per process, mostly empty. How do real systems make it smaller? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "A flat page table is <em>4 MB</em>. How do we shrink it?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Chop the flat table into page sized pieces, and add a directory of pieces. If a whole piece would be empty, don't allocate it: mark its directory entry invalid. A process using a few megabytes, scattered across a huge address space, then needs only a handful of table pages. x86-64 uses four levels, for 48 bit addresses: nine bits index each level, and twelve bits are the offset. C R three points to the top level. Each entry points to the next table, until the last level gives the frame. The catch: a TLB miss now costs four memory reads, not one. CPUs cache the upper levels, in page walk caches, and the TLB hides most of the rest.",
          "props": {
            "title": "A tree of <em>tables</em>",
            "nodes": [
