    {
      "id": "04",
      "title": "Huge pages",
      "question": "When do huge pages help, and why do some databases say to turn them off?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question four. When do huge pages help? And why do some databases tell you to turn them off? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "When do <em>huge pages</em> help, and when do they hurt?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "TLB reach is entries times page size. Fifteen hundred entries of four kilobytes cover six megabytes. With two megabyte pages, the same entries cover three gigabytes. A huge page also ends the walk one level early. So workloads with big, randomly accessed memory, like databases, JVM heaps, and trading engines, take far fewer misses.",
          "props": {
            "title": "TLB <em>reach</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "reach",
                "label": "TLB reach",
                "title": "Entries × page size",
                "body": "1,536 × 4 KB = 6 MB · × 2 MB = 3 GB.",
                "code": "second-level TLB"
              },
              {
                "at": "early",
                "label": "Shorter walk",
                "title": "One level fewer",
                "body": "The PD entry maps 2 MB directly.",
                "code": "3 reads, not 4"
              },
              {
                "at": "randomly",
                "label": "Who benefits",
                "title": "Big, random working sets",
                "body": "Databases, JVM heaps, trading engines.",
                "code": "perf stat -e dTLB-load-misses"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Static huge pages are reserved up front, and requested explicitly: predictable, but you manage the pool. Transparent huge pages are automatic: the kernel uses two megabyte pages when it can, and a background thread collapses small pages later. The catch is latency. Finding two contiguous megabytes can mean compacting memory, in the middle of an allocation. And after a fork, copy on write copies two megabytes for a one byte write. That's why Redis says to disable them, and why many latency-sensitive systems set them to madvise, opting in per region.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Static or <em>transparent</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "Static",
              "label": "Static",
              "title": "Reserved, explicit",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "hugetlbfs or MAP_HUGETLB. Predictable; you size the pool."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "Transparent",
              "label": "Transparent (THP)",
              "title": "Automatic, with stalls",
              "body": "Compaction stalls; after fork, COW copies 2 MB per write."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "opting",
              "text": "Latency-sensitive: THP = madvise, opt in per region.",
              "code": "madvise(p, n, MADV_HUGEPAGE)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. The TLB caches translations, and locality makes it hit almost always. On a context switch, tagged entries avoid a full flush. Multi level tables only allocate what's used, at the cost of a longer walk. And huge pages multiply TLB reach, but transparent ones can add latency spikes.",
          "props": {
            "title": "TLBs, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "caches",
                "text": "The TLB caches translations; locality makes it hit.",
                "code": "spatial · temporal"
              },
              {
                "at": "tagged",
                "text": "ASID / PCID tags avoid flushing on a switch.",
                "code": "shootdowns on unmap"
              },
              {
                "at": "allocate",
                "text": "Multi-level tables allocate only what's used.",
                "code": "4 levels, 9 bits each"
              },
              {
                "at": "multiply",
                "text": "Huge pages multiply TLB reach.",
                "code": "THP: watch the latency"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
